WEBVTT

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:18.590
Thankyou.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:22.009
Well, it has been a great pleasure to meet
you Sam, and Lin.

00:00:22.009 --> 00:00:24.500
Yes, we've enjoyed meeting you too Victor.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.109
Yes, it's been great. What a pity you have
to go home.

00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:32.610
Well, all good things must come to an end.
But I'm sure we'll meet again.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.410
Yes, I hope so.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:38.360
And good luck with your business. I'm sure
it will go well.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.920
And I wish you every success too.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.830
Well, I think we should drink a toast to the
end of the conference, and to ourselves. Here's

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.920
to us.
Cheers

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:48.920
Cheers

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.000
Cheers.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.430
We should keep in touch.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:58.600
Yes. Have I given you my card?

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.379
No - thanks very much.
Here's mine.

00:01:02.379 --> 00:01:06.229
Do you have a card Lin?

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:07.229
Yes.

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:13.930
Thank you. I'll send you an email. And if
you're ever in Singapore, you must look me

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:14.930
up.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:18.461
We certainly will. And you have my number.
When you're next in Sydney, give me a call

00:01:18.461 --> 00:01:20.110
- we'll have a drink.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:23.640
May I take these?

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.150
Well, I'd better get going or I'll miss my
flight.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:29.610
Have a good flight home. Bon voyage.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:37.140
Goodbye. Until next time.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.990
Goodbye.
For the final program in the series we're

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:46.950
looking at some of the phrases you may use
when you're saying goodbye to someone - either

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:54.810
for a short time, or a long time. In our example,
Victor is from another country, and he's about

00:01:54.810 --> 00:02:00.270
to go back home. At a conference, he's met
Sam and Lin.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.950
It has been a great pleasure to meet you Sam
and Lin.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.840
We've enjoyed meeting you too Victor.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.500
Yes, it's been great. What a pity you have
to go home.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:15.790
There are various phrases you can use to express
how enjoyable it was to meet someone. Which

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:22.120
one you use depends on how well you got to
know them. Practise some of these phrases

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.760
with Victor.
It's been a great pleasure to meet you.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:33.709
I have enjoyed meeting you.

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:37.730
I'm so glad to have met you.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:44.010
Nice to meet you.
The phrase 'nice to meet you' would be used

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:51.459
after one short meeting. You can also use
this phrase when you are introduced to someone.

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:55.870
What about the replies? Practise them with
Lin.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:00.580
Nice to meet you.
You too.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:06.440
It's been a pleasure to meet you.
And you.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:12.640
Glad to have met you.
Glad to have met you too.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.819
Notice that the reply should match the statement.
So if someone says: I have enjoyed meeting

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:23.250
you, the reply can be, So have I.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:29.260
If someone says, "It's been a pleasure to
meet you," the reply can be, "A pleasure to

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:37.110
meet you too", or just "And you". Victor also
says, "I'm sure we'll meet again."

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:44.129
Here are some useful phrases to do with meeting
again. Practise them with Victor.

00:03:44.129 --> 00:03:48.019
I'm sure we'll meet again.

00:03:48.019 --> 00:03:49.180
Hopefully we'll meet again.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:58.760
I hope we'll meet again soon.
Notice again here that the reply should match

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:05.769
the statement, so if someone says, "I'm sure
we'll meet again," then the reply also uses

00:04:05.769 --> 00:04:14.519
'am': So am I. After the statement, "I hope
we'll meet again", the reply should be, "So

00:04:14.519 --> 00:04:17.180
do I".

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:21.608
Another part of saying goodbye can be wishing
someone well for the future.

00:04:21.608 --> 00:04:25.560
And good luck with your business. I'm sure
it will go well.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:32.380
And I wish you every success too.
Sam proposes a toast. Watch how he does this.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:37.190
Well, I think we should drink a toast to the
end of the conference, and to ourselves. Here's

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.300
to us. Cheers.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.300
Cheers.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:46.250
Cheers.
This is an informal toast. Sam says, "I think

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:48.880
we should drink a toast".

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.300
Another phrase he could use is 'Let's drink
to'. For example, 'Let's drink to the end

00:04:54.300 --> 00:05:00.090
of the conference' or 'Let's drink to our
future meeting'. Then they clink their glasses

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.969
together and say 'Cheers'.

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:08.159
Here's another version of the toast:
Let's drink to our next meeting.

00:05:08.159 --> 00:05:09.159
Our next meeting!

00:05:09.159 --> 00:05:15.110
Cheers
And of course, the toast doesn't have to be

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:19.899
alcohol, it can be any kind of drink.

00:05:19.899 --> 00:05:26.080
The next part of their conversation is about
keeping in touch, or keeping in contact.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:27.729
We should keep in touch.

00:05:27.729 --> 00:05:32.000
Yes. Have I given you my card?

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.590
No - thanks very much. Here's mine.

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:39.409
Do you have a card Lin?

00:05:39.409 --> 00:05:40.409
Yes.

00:05:40.409 --> 00:05:47.940
Thank you. I'll send you an email.
Repeat the phrases after Sam.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:51.930
We must keep in touch.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:56.380
We must keep in contact.

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:59.630
Here's my card.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:01.720
Would you like my card?

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:08.700
Do you have a card?
The next part of their conversation is about

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.850
meeting again. Listen:
And if you're ever in Singapore, you must

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:13.850
look me up.

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:18.001
We certainly will. And you have my number.
When you're next in Sydney, give me a call

00:06:18.001 --> 00:06:22.090
- we'll have a drink.
To 'look someone up' just means to arrange

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:29.219
a meeting. When Victor says, "You must look
me up", he is inviting Sam and Lin to meet

00:06:29.219 --> 00:06:36.279
him if they are in Singapore. This is more
of a social invitation, than a business one.

00:06:36.279 --> 00:06:41.280
Using the word 'must' is not like an order
here - it suggests that Victor will be very

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:45.399
happy if Sam sees him in Singapore.

00:06:45.399 --> 00:06:51.960
In the same way, Sam says, "Give me a call"
to Victor. It sounds like an order, but in

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:59.270
fact it's an invitation. It's important to
get the intonation - the way you say it - right,

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:03.080
so that it sounds like an invitation, and
not an order.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:05.380
You must look me up when you're in Singapore.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:06.890
And if I don't?

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:13.469
I'll never speak to you again!
Practise these kinds of invitations with Victor.

00:07:13.469 --> 00:07:19.940
You must look me up next time you're in town.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:24.250
You must come and see me.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:29.640
Why don't you give me a call when you're in
town?

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:36.279
Ring me if you're in town.
Finally let's look at how the three friends

00:07:36.279 --> 00:07:41.849
say goodbye. Remember this is a semi-formal
situation.

00:07:41.849 --> 00:07:44.349
Well, I'd better get going or I'll miss my
flight.

00:07:44.349 --> 00:07:47.810
Have a good flight home. Bon voyage.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:50.930
Goodbye. Until next time.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:54.490
Goodbye.
There are a few ways of saying goodbye but

00:07:54.490 --> 00:08:02.659
the simplest and best is simply 'good bye'.
Sam says "bon voyage" - a French phrase which

00:08:02.659 --> 00:08:07.510
is also quite common for someone who is travelling.

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:11.580
Now, let's review and practise some of the
phrases we've learnt today.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:16.380
It's been a pleasure to meet you.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:20.570
I'm sure we'll meet again.

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:21.870
We must keep in touch.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:29.070
Give me a call when you're in town.

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:32.740
I've enjoyed meeting you.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:35.000
I wish you every success for the future.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:41.080
May I give you my card?

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:42.390
Best wishes for the future.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:51.950
I hope you have a good flight home.
The language you use in each situation may

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:56.880
be slightly different depending on how well
you know the other people, and how friendly

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:03.360
you are with them. If the situation is social,
and you have become quite friendly, you may

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:09.630
use slightly less formal language. But it's
important not to forget the usual expressions

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:15.690
of good wishes - such as for a good flight
home, and to say how you've enjoyed meeting

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:21.990
the other person. But don't go too far.
Well, I'd better get going or I'll miss my

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:23.210
flight.

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:25.270
I'm going to miss you Victor.

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:28.450
So am I. What will we do without you?

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:29.640
Be strong.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:31.010
Will we meet again?

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:35.120
I know we will.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:36.120
Goodbye.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:37.670
Goodbye Victor - and bon voyage.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:44.360
You forgot my card!
Well, I've enjoyed helping you with The Business

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:49.399
of English, and I hope you've enjoyed learning
some useful phrases and expressions in English

00:09:49.399 --> 00:09:52.890
- and that you'll be able to put them into
practice soon.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.780
Goodbye and good luck!